  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "Consider the statement, “If \\(n\\) is not prime, then \\(n-2\\) is prime.” Which of the following values of \\(n\\) is a counterexample to this statement?",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\( 11 \\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\( 15 \\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\( 19 \\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\( 21 \\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\( 27 \\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "e",
    "value": "27"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10b-2019-p2.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "We need \\(n\\) to not be prime, so \\(n\\) can only be \\(15,21,27.\\) Then, \\(n-2\\) must be not prime, leaving just \\(27.\\)\n\nThus, the answer is E .",
      "hints": [
        "A counterexample must make the hypothesis true and conclusion false",
        "Check only choices where \\(n\\) is not prime"
      ],
